**[[obj:intercostalnerves]]** are nerves that run within the intercostal spaces [1][2]. In the thorax, they lie in the **[[obj:costalgroove]]** along the inferior margin of the superior rib, between the inner two intercostal muscle layers [2]. They are situated deep to the **[[obj:externalintercostalmuscles]]** and **[[obj:internalintercostalmuscles]]**, while the **[[obj:innermostintercostalmuscles]]** are the deepest layer [5][6].

They carry:
- somatic motor fibers to thoracic wall muscles, including the **[[obj:externalintercostalmuscles]]**, **[[obj:internalintercostalmuscles]]**, **[[obj:innermostintercostalmuscles]]**, and **[[obj:transversusthoracismuscle]]** [1][5];
- sensory fibers from skin and the **[[obj:pleura]]** [1];
- postganglionic sympathetic fibers [1].

The lower **[[obj:intercostalnerves]]** (T7–T11) continue onto the anterolateral abdominal wall between the internal oblique and **[[obj:transversusabdominismuscle]]**, then pass posterior to the **[[obj:rectusabdominismuscle]]** and give anterior cutaneous branches to the skin [4]. The subcostal nerve and the **[[obj:iliohypogastricnerve]]** and **[[obj:ilioinguinalnerve]]** follow related courses [4].

